<!-- ============ SECTION 3 · THE MAY PIVOT + DIAGNOSTIC ============ -->
<tr>
<td style="padding:32px 36px; border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">
<h2 style="margin:0 0 16px 0; font-size:22px; font-weight:900; color:#232F3E; letter-spacing:-0.3px; line-height:1.3; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">The May 2026 pivot — why "data quality" is the new headline</h2>

<p style="margin:0 0 16px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">The <a href="https://www.swift.com/swift-resource/252556/download" style="color:#0972D3; font-weight:700; text-decoration:none;">April PMPG insights</a> said: validation is moving to point of entry. The <a href="https://www.swift.com/swift-resource/252560/download" style="color:#0972D3; font-weight:700; text-decoration:none;">May insights</a> said: structured fields may be populated and still be wrong. Those two statements, together, are the industry update.</p>

<p style="margin:0 0 16px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">Pre-2026, the operating assumption was that a well-formatted message — schema-valid, fields in the right place — would clear. Downstream repair existed as a safety net. The April update removed the safety net. The May insights then pointed out that "well-formatted" was always a weaker claim than the industry treated it as.</p>

