<!-- ============ SECTION 4 · REGULATORY WATCH — WHERE REGULATION FALLS ============ -->
<tr>
<td style="padding:32px 36px; border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">
<p style="margin:0 0 12px 0; font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:2px; text-transform:uppercase; color:#0972D3;">Regulatory Watch</p>
<h2 style="margin:0 0 16px 0; font-size:22px; font-weight:900; color:#232F3E; letter-spacing:-0.3px; line-height:1.3; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">Where the regulation falls — and where the data lives</h2>

<p style="margin:0 0 16px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">PMPG guidance is addressed to banks. Banks are the SWIFT-regulated party — they carry the November 2026 exposure. But the data that determines whether a payment is compliant is set upstream, in the corporate's ERP or treasury management system.</p>

<p style="margin:0 0 16px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">That asymmetry is the operating reality: the regulation sits on one side of the relationship; the data source sits on the other. No single party can deliver the cutover alone — and crucially, no single path will. Each side has two paths to choose from. The institutions that combine them are the ones that ship clean in November.</p>

<p style="margin:0 0 16px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">The <a href="https://www.swift.com/swift-resource/252512/download" style="color:#0972D3; font-weight:700; text-decoration:none;">Joint Call to Action to Corporates</a> and the <a href="https://www.swift.com/swift-resource/252560/download" style="color:#0972D3; font-weight:700; text-decoration:none;">May "Mission Possible" insights</a> both explicitly frame the residual cutover risk as a shared bank-corporate responsibility — the first time PMPG guidance has treated corporates as <strong style="color:#232F3E;">co-deliverers of the cutover</strong>. What follows is the operating framework that responsibility implies.</p>

<!-- Pull quote callout -->
<table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0;">
<tr>
<td style="border-left:3px solid #43ADC1; background-color:#F5F9FF; border:1px solid #D0DAEB; border-radius:4px; padding:16px 20px;">
<p style="margin:0; font-size:15px; line-height:1.5; color:#466587; font-style:italic;">Two sides. Four paths. The cutover belongs to the institutions that combine them.</p>
</td>
</tr>
</table>
</td>
</tr>

<!-- ============ SECTION 5 · THE FOUR-PATH FRAMEWORK ============ -->
<tr>
<td style="padding:32px 36px; border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">
<h2 style="margin:0 0 16px 0; font-size:22px; font-weight:900; color:#232F3E; letter-spacing:-0.3px; line-height:1.3; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">The four-path framework</h2>

<p style="margin:0 0 16px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">One acknowledgement before the framework. Restructuring master data inside a Tier-1 corporate ERP is a multi-year programme — typically seven to eight figures in cost, touching every downstream system that consumes that master data, and constrained by procurement cycles that do not bend to PMPG deadlines. Many corporates cannot complete it by November 2026. <strong style="color:#232F3E;">That is a real constraint, not an excuse.</strong> The framework below assumes it: each side has both a structural path and a faster path; the right combination is institution-specific; and "compliant by November" does not require every party to take the heaviest lift.</p>

<p style="margin:0 0 16px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">The bank's two paths sit in different registers. Channel-side validation is the regulated floor every bank must build by November. Enrichment and pre-check services are the strategic ceiling each bank can choose to build above it. Most institutions will combine both — and combine them with one or both of the corporate paths.</p>

<!-- Figure 3 image -->
<table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:8px 0 12px 0;">
<tr>
<td align="center" style="line-height:0; font-size:0;">
<img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dsr6vlfn1/image/upload/q_auto/f_auto/v1780552132/image007_e4cx6b.png"
width="528" alt="Figure 3 — The four-path framework. Corporate side: Path 1 Master-Data Restructure (strategic) and Path 2 Transaction-Time Enrichment (tactical). Bank side: Path 3 Channel-Side Validation (regulated floor) and Path 4 Enrichment and Pre-Check Service (strategic ceiling)."
border="0" style="display:block; width:100%; max-width:528px; height:auto; border:1px solid #E8E8E8; border-radius:4px;">
</td>
</tr>
