<!-- PATH 1 -->
<table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 16px 0;">
<tr>
<td style="background-color:#F0F4F9; border:1px solid #D0DAEB; border-radius:6px; padding:20px 24px;">
<p style="margin:0 0 4px 0; font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:2px; text-transform:uppercase; color:#0972D3;">Path 1 · Corporate · Strategic</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 10px 0; font-size:17px; font-weight:900; color:#232F3E;">Master-Data Restructure</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 12px 0; font-size:15px; line-height:1.6; color:#232F3E;">The structural fix for corporates whose ERP or TMS programme can deliver it. Audit how master data captures town and country today — a surprising number of corporates discover, on first audit, that town and country live concatenated inside a single free-text address string. That is the gap. Capture town name and country as discrete, validated fields — ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 for country, maximum 35 characters for town. Every downstream system that touches master data needs the same discipline.</p>
<p style="margin:0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.6; color:#466587; font-style:italic;"><strong style="color:#466587;">Realistic for:</strong> institutions with active 2026 ERP/TMS programmes. &nbsp;·&nbsp; <strong style="color:#466587;">Not realistic:</strong> longer timelines — see Path 2.</p>
</td>
</tr>
</table>

<!-- PATH 2 -->
<table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 16px 0;">
<tr>
<td style="background-color:#F0F4F9; border:1px solid #D0DAEB; border-radius:6px; padding:20px 24px;">
<p style="margin:0 0 4px 0; font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:2px; text-transform:uppercase; color:#0972D3;">Path 2 · Corporate · Tactical</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 10px 0; font-size:17px; font-weight:900; color:#232F3E;">Transaction-Time Enrichment</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 12px 0; font-size:15px; line-height:1.6; color:#232F3E;">The faster path for corporates whose master data will not be ready by November. Enrich the address on the outbound payment, leaving the underlying master-data record alone. Two operational moves. First, migrate from MT101 to pain.001 SCORE where your house bank supports it — the <a href="https://www.swift.com/swift-resource/252289/download" style="color:#0972D3; font-weight:700; text-decoration:none;">March PMPG update</a> is direct: as of SR2026, the MT101 address option is treated as Name only. Keep MT101 with address information and you lose the address. Second, push the same discipline to payment factories and service bureaus. Their channel is your channel for compliance purposes.</p>
<p style="margin:0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.6; color:#466587; font-style:italic;"><strong style="color:#466587;">Realistic for:</strong> corporates with mature payment infrastructure but constrained master-data programmes. &nbsp;·&nbsp; <strong style="color:#466587;">Not realistic:</strong> corporates whose payment factories cannot operate a new enrichment layer.</p>
</td>
</tr>
</table>

<!-- PATH 3 -->
<table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 16px 0;">
<tr>
<td style="background-color:#FFFBF2; border:1px solid #F5DEB3; border-radius:6px; padding:20px 24px;">
<p style="margin:0 0 4px 0; font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:2px; text-transform:uppercase; color:#B8860B;">Path 3 · Bank · Regulated Floor</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 10px 0; font-size:17px; font-weight:900; color:#232F3E;">Channel-Side Validation</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 12px 0; font-size:15px; line-height:1.6; color:#232F3E;">The bank's regulatory obligation under <a href="https://www.swift.com/swift-resource/252519/download" style="color:#0972D3; font-weight:700; text-decoration:none;">PMPG Guide Dec 2025</a>. Three primary actions, all required by November. Enforce town name and country structurally — as hard validation, not as preferred fields. Reject duplicated postal content at the channel boundary pre-submission: if the corporate sends a town name in the structured field and again in an address line, the channel must reject it. Handle cover-leg messages without modifying or downgrading the underlying customer credit transfer's hybrid addresses.</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 12px 0; font-size:15px; line-height:1.6; color:#232F3E;">Two configuration details worth noting in the same pass. The Legal Entity Identifier is complementary, not substitutive — the <a href="https://www.bis.org/cpmi/pietf/iso20022_2026.pdf" style="color:#0972D3; font-weight:700; text-decoration:none;">CPMI/PIE ISO 20022 Harmonisation Panel</a> clarified in February 2026 that it is an additional identifier alongside name and postal address, not a replacement. And an agent's postal address is "not desired" per the <a href="https://www.bis.org/cpmi/publ/d230_annex.pdf" style="color:#0972D3; font-weight:700; text-decoration:none;">February BIS guidance</a> — use the agent's business identifier code with clearing-system member ID and LEI instead. If your message-generation pipeline emits agent postal addresses by default, that is a configuration change before November.</p>
<p style="margin:0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.6; color:#466587; font-style:italic;"><strong style="color:#466587;">Realistic for:</strong> every bank operating CBPR+ corridors — this is the regulated floor. &nbsp;·&nbsp; <strong style="color:#466587;">Not realistic:</strong> no exemptions. The institutions that stop here are still compliant. They are not yet competitive.</p>
</td>
</tr>
</table>

<!-- PATH 4 -->
<table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 20px 0;">
<tr>
<td style="background-color:#F1F8F2; border:1px solid #C8E6C9; border-radius:6px; padding:20px 24px;">
<p style="margin:0 0 4px 0; font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:2px; text-transform:uppercase; color:#2E7D32;">Path 4 · Bank · Strategic Service Line</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 10px 0; font-size:17px; font-weight:900; color:#232F3E;">Enrichment &amp; Pre-Check Service</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 12px 0; font-size:15px; line-height:1.6; color:#232F3E;">The ceiling — what banks can build above the regulated floor. Offer enrichment and pre-check as a service to corporate clients: take unstructured corporate input, return structured output, before the payment is submitted for execution. This is the path that turns the November 2026 compliance moment into a multi-year service line — the same capability serves CBPR+ today, sanctions-list expansion next year, ESG payment metadata after that. Two implementation moves matter from day one. Publish your corporate ingestion specification in writing — the <a href="https://www.swift.com/swift-resource/252289/download" style="color:#0972D3; font-weight:700; text-decoration:none;">PMPG March update</a> calls this the financial institution's obligation to equip clients with updated payment specifications. And instrument inter-field consistency monitoring as a monthly dashboard metric — this is the metric the <a href="https://www.swift.com/swift-resource/252560/download" style="color:#0972D3; font-weight:700; text-decoration:none;">PMPG May insights</a> are asking for in everything but name.</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 12px 0; font-size:15px; line-height:1.6; color:#232F3E;">The bank-side enrichment and pre-check category is the part of the framework where a 2026 build decision becomes a ten-year platform decision. <strong style="color:#232F3E;">This is the category ioNova ARS was built for, and the reason it exists.</strong></p>
<p style="margin:0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.6; color:#466587; font-style:italic;"><strong style="color:#466587;">Realistic for:</strong> any bank with the engineering capacity to operate a real-time service alongside the channel. &nbsp;·&nbsp; <strong style="color:#466587;">Not realistic:</strong> banks treating CBPR+ as a one-off compliance project. The former see the ceiling. The latter do not.</p>
</td>
</tr>
</table>

<!-- Operational caveat callout -->
<table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 20px 0;">
<tr>
<td style="border-left:3px solid #43ADC1; background-color:#F5F9FF; border:1px solid #D0DAEB; border-radius:4px; padding:16px 20px;">
<p style="margin:0 0 8px 0; font-size:15px; line-height:1.6; color:#232F3E;"><strong style="color:#232F3E;">One operational caveat: CUTOVER2026 is not a wildcard.</strong></p>
<p style="margin:0; font-size:15px; line-height:1.6; color:#232F3E;">The <a href="https://www.swift.com/swift-resource/252289/download" style="color:#0972D3; font-weight:700; text-decoration:none;">March update</a> permits the CUTOVER2026 keyword in the town-name field for in-flight pre-cutover transactions only, between 15 November 2026 and approximately 15 February 2027. Newly initiated payments cannot use it. Use it as a wildcard for messy data and your 2027 compliance review will be expensive. Plan for the keyword to disappear from your message-generation pipeline by Q1 2027.</p>
</td>
